{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.OrthoschemePolarReduction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.OrthoschemePolarReduction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.OrthoschemeBaseIntegral","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolumeFubini","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.PolarCoord"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.hypVolume_chimney_orthoBase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.hypVolume_chimney_orthoBase","kind":"theorem","line":412,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L412-L432","doc":"**The hyperbolic volume of the chimney over one orthoscheme base triangle\nis `½Λ(ψ)`.**\n\nThe first statement in this chain with a THREE-dimensional hyperbolic volume on\nthe left and `Λ` on the right.  H4-2 collapses the solid to its base integral,\nH4-3 turns that into Milnor's angular integral, H4-4 evaluates it.\n\n**This is not a statement about an ideal tetrahedron.**  It says that a\nparticular region of `H³` — the region above the radius-`R` hemisphere over an\nopen Euclidean right triangle — has hyperbolic volume `½Λ(ψ)`.  That such a\nchimney over a triangle INSCRIBED in the circle IS the ideal tetrahedron with\ndihedral angles `(α,β,γ)` is Milnor's geometric identification, and it is\nuntouched: Mathlib has no ideal tetrahedra.  Assembling six of these into\n`Λ(α)+Λ(β)+Λ(γ)` is H4-5, also open — and that step is where the obtuse case\n(circumcentre outside the triangle, one piece entering with a minus sign) finally\nhas to be faced. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.orthoBase_subset_disk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.orthoBase_subset_disk","kind":"theorem","line":384,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L384-L410","doc":"The open base triangle lies strictly inside the disk.\n\nIts third vertex `(R cos ψ, R sin ψ)` is ON the circle, but the triangle is open,\nso no point of it is: `x² + y² < x²(1 + tan²ψ) = x²/cos²ψ < R²`.  This is the\npointwise disk hypothesis H4-2's corollary wants, so the a.e. form is not needed\nfor a single orthoscheme — it will be, for the closed triangle H4-5 assembles. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.lintegral_orthoBase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.lintegral_orthoBase","kind":"theorem","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L232-L376","doc":"**H4-3 — the base integral over one orthoscheme triangle collapses to\nMilnor's angular integral.**\n\n    ∫∫_{orthoBase R ψ} dA / (2(R² − x² − y²))\n        =  ∫₀^ψ −¼ log((cos²φ − cos²ψ)/cos²φ) dφ   ( = ½Λ(ψ), by H4-4 )\n\nThree things happen here, and each is where the corresponding hypothesis is used:\n\n* the polar change of variables (`lintegral_comp_polarCoord_symm`), whose\n  Jacobian `r` is what makes the radial primitive a logarithm;\n* Tonelli with the ANGLE outermost, so the inner integral is the radial one —\n  the order Milnor computes in;\n* the radial evaluation, whose upper limit `R cos ψ / cos φ` is `< R` exactly\n  because `φ < ψ` (the chord stays inside the circle).\n\nThe right-hand side carries no `R`: the radial value depends on `r₀/R`, and\n`r₀/R = cos ψ / cos φ`.  That is H2's dilation invariance visible in the base\nintegral. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.lintegral_radial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.lintegral_radial","kind":"theorem","line":212,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L212-L225","doc":"**The radial mass of one polar ray**, in `ℝ≥0∞`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.mem_orthoBase_polar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.mem_orthoBase_polar","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L134-L203","doc":"**The polar description of the base triangle** — proved, not assumed.\n\nFor `r > 0` and `φ ∈ (−π, π)`, the point `(r cos φ, r sin φ)` lies in the\ntriangle exactly when `0 < φ < ψ` and `r < R cos ψ / cos φ`.  This is the step\nthat would have been smuggled in by defining the region in polar coordinates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.measurableSet_orthoBase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.measurableSet_orthoBase","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L131-L132","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.isOpen_orthoBase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.isOpen_orthoBase","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L121-L129","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.orthoBase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.orthoBase","kind":"def","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L112-L119","doc":"**The orthoscheme base triangle** — the OPEN Euclidean triangle with vertices\n`(0,0)`, `(R cos ψ, 0)`, `(R cos ψ, R sin ψ)`: the circumcentre, the foot of the\nperpendicular to a chord, and that chord's endpoint on the circle of radius `R`.\n\nOne of the six pieces Milnor's circumcentre subdivision cuts an inscribed\ntriangle into. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_radial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_radial","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L74-L106","doc":"**`∫₀^{r₀} r dr / (2(R² − r²)) = −¼ log((R² − r₀²)/R²)`** for `0 ≤ r₀ < R`.\n\nThe polar Jacobian `r` against the density `1/(2(R²−r²))` integrates to a log —\nwhich is why the angular integrand of the orthoscheme is a `log`, and hence why\n`Λ` (the primitive of `−log(2 sin)`) is the answer at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_radial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_radial","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemePolarReduction.lean#L61-L72","doc":"The radial integrand is continuous on `[0, r₀]` when `r₀ < R`, hence\ninterval-integrable there.  Unlike the angular integrand, there is no singularity\ninside the interval: the chord stays strictly inside the circle. ","signatureChunk":0}]}